   "text": "This is a rifamycin-type ansamycin in which the naphthoquinone core is fused to a phenoxazine ring. The phenoxazine carries a 2-(4-methylpiperazin-1-yl)ethoxy substituent. I built the SMILES region by region from the drawing. The stereocentres and alkene geometries on the ansa chain were adapted from the natural rifamycin pattern, which I recalled from memory rather than reading each wedge and hash individually, so they are the least certain part.\n\n- **Core ring system:** the furanone ring has a quaternary carbon (methyl, ring oxygen, enol-ether oxygen bridge) and a C=O. It is fused to the aromatic ring bearing a methyl and an OH. That ring is fused to the quinone ring (C=O and NH-amide). The quinone ring is in turn fused to the phenoxazine (N on the carbon next to the furanone side, O on the carbon next to the NH-bearing carbon).\n- **Phenoxazine substituent:** the benzo ring carries OCH2CH2\u2013N(piperazine)\u2013N-methyl.\n- **Ansa chain, from the amide C=O onward:** C(Me)=CH\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH(Me)\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH(Me)\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH(Me)\u2013CH(OAc)\u2013CH(Me)\u2013CH(OMe)\u2013CH=CH\u2013O, closing onto the quaternary carbon.\n\nI checked the ring closures and atom counts against the drawing.\n\nSMILES: CO[C@H]1/C=C/O[C@@]2(C)Oc3c(C)c(O)c4c(c3C2=O)c3nc5ccc(OCCN6CCN(C)CC6)cc5oc3c(NC(=O)/C(C)=C\\C=C\\[C@H](C)[C@H](O)[C@@H](C)[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)[C@H](OC(C)=O)[C@@H]1C)c4=O"
  }
